  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nFor now, it's beginning to look as though the anomalous heat and \nhumidity will give way to the seasonal transition over the course of \nthe next week. This cool down may seem cool or even chilly to some, \nas we shave 20-30 degrees from both high and low temperatures for \nseveral days. \n\nSurface high pressure and mid-level ridging develop over the Great \nLakes into the southeastern Canadian Provinces as the axis to an \nupper ridge over the Plains edge eastward toward the Mississippi \nRiver Valley. Global guidance (ECM/GFS) both show some version of \nthis progression, though they begin to diverge in its evolution from \nmid to late week. GFS shows the upper wave getting pinched off and \nmomentarily becoming stationary west of Lake Michigan, while ECM is \nmore progressive, taking the upper wave into the Ohio Valley. In \nboth scenarios, easterly flow is funneled into the region with \nlimited moisture and mid-level temperatures in the single digits to \naround 10C. Despite some of the spread in the long range synoptic \nevolution, surface temperature spreads are pretty reasonable, \nrunning around 5 degrees through much of the period. Additionally, \nlong range teleconnection outlooks give a bit of a boost in a shift \ntoward sustaining fall-like temperatures as projections show the PNA \nsharply trending more positive and AO favoring negative trends. This \nwould lean in favor of eastern CONUS troughing with northwesterly \nflow maintaining more of a fall-like feel through the later part of \nthe month into early October.",
